---
// /colophon: "How this site is made" (Featherboa, "The dares," ix). The faces
// and why, what each color means, and the site's promises about contrast,
// motion, and reading without JavaScript, so it is also the site's
// accessibility statement. Every fact follows the site's code and the record
// (src/pages/CLAUDE.md, "The colophon"); test/colophon.test.js holds the
// faces and the colors to their files.
//
// "Who makes this" carries queue Q2's sentence about the AI, as Claude drafted
// it. It is BVJ's to word, keep, or drop (SITE-FEATHERBOA-BUILD.md, Q2); if it
// stays, the sentence after it says who translates the text, and the two stay
// together.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/pages/colophon.css";

const title = "How this site is made | Liberation and Inclusion Translation";
const description =
  "The typefaces, colors, and promises behind the LIT Bible website: contrast, motion, and reading without JavaScript. It is also our accessibility statement.";

const colors = [
  {
    name: "Paper",
    hex: "#FAFAF8",
    swatch: "var(--brand-paper)",
    job: "The page. Every page is this one color.",
  },
  {
    name: "Ink",
    hex: "#1D231C",
    swatch: "var(--brand-ink)",
    job: "All words. The grays you see are ink at a lighter strength, never a separate gray.",
  },
  {
    name: "LIT Green",
    hex: "#209D50",
    swatch: "var(--brand-lit-green)",
    job: "A mark: the emblem, the chapter number, “you are here.” It is never behind small words, and never the color of words.",
  },
  {
    name: "Deep green",
    hex: "#0F6B33",
    swatch: "var(--brand-deep-green)",
    job: "What you can press: links, verse numbers, footnote letters, and the one main button on a page.",
  },
  {
    name: "Sunshine",
    hex: "#FFD23F",
    swatch: "var(--brand-sunshine)",
    job: "Yours: what you highlight and mark. (Highlights also come in green, blue, and pink.)",
  },
  {
    name: "Red",
    hex: "#B3261E",
    swatch: "var(--brand-red)",
    job: "Deleting, errors, and bookmarks.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://litbible.net/colophon/"
>
  <article class="colophon-page">
    <div class="colophon-page__inner">
      <header>
        <h1 class="colophon-title" id="colophon-title">How this site is made</h1>
        <p class="colophon-lede">
          This page says how the LIT Bible website is built and what it
          promises you as a reader. It is also our accessibility statement:
          what we have done so that people with different eyes, hands, and
          devices can read the LIT, and how to tell us when we fall short.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-who">
        <h2 id="colophon-who">Who makes it</h2>
        {/* Queue Q2: BVJ's to word, keep, or drop. Keep these two sentences together. */}
        <p>
          People make every decision about this site, and much of its code is
          written with Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic. The translation
          is not: Brandon translates every word of the LIT.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-type">
        <h2 id="colophon-type">The type</h2>
        <p>Three typefaces, each with one job.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Fraunces</strong> for titles and headings. It is a serif
            with soft, slightly quirky shapes, and it gives the site its voice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Source Sans 3</strong> for everything you read at length:
            scripture, footnotes, introductions, articles, and the glossary.
            Its Greek letters are drawn for it, so the Greek matches the
            English around it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Atkinson Hyperlegible Next</strong> for buttons, menus, and
            small labels. It was designed so that letters people often confuse
            look different from one another.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Why no serif for reading?</strong> The British Dyslexia
          Association recommends sans-serif faces for readers with dyslexia,
          and one study of adults with dyslexia found them easier to read. The
          research is thin and mixed, and it finds no difference in reading
          speed for readers in general, so the choice costs no one anything. Serifs
          stay in headings, where they are short and large.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your choices.</strong> The “Aa” button in the header opens
          Display, where you can change the text size (four steps), the space
          between lines, and the typeface. Besides the default, you can choose
          Atkinson Hyperlegible for all text, or OpenDyslexic, a typeface
          designed for readers with dyslexia. Your choices are kept on your own
          device.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-color">
        <h2 id="colophon-color">Color</h2>
        <p>
          Every color has one job, so a color tells you something. When green
          means “you can press this,” you learn it on your first page.
        </p>
        <dl class="colophon-colors">
          {colors.map((c) => (
            <div class="colophon-color">
              <dt>
                <span class="colophon-swatch" style={`background-color: ${c.swatch}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {c.name} <span class="colophon-hex">{c.hex}</span>
              </dt>
              <dd>{c.job}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          <strong>At night</strong> the page is a black with a little green in
          it (<span class="colophon-hex">#111411</span>), and the words are a
          soft white (<span class="colophon-hex">#ECEEE8</span>), because pure
          white on pure black glares. Green gets brighter and highlights get
          softer. Choose Light, Dark, or your device’s own setting in Display.
          Without JavaScript, the site follows your device.
        </p>
        <p>
          Color never carries meaning alone: a tab or setting that is on is
          also heavier, not only a different color.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-contrast">
        <h2 id="colophon-contrast">Contrast, focus, and size</h2>
        <p>
          We measure contrast with the standard formula (the one behind WCAG,
          the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Our promises, by day and
          by night:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Words stand out from the page behind them by at
            least 4.5 to 1</strong>, by day and by night, in every state of a
            control. Scripture is about 15 to 1. A button that is
            unavailable stays readable: it never fades away.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Edges you need to see, and the focus ring, are at least 3
            to 1.</strong> LIT Green is used only for marks and large numbers,
            which is why green words are always the deeper green.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>If you use a keyboard, you can always see where you
            are.</strong> One ring, the same everywhere, draws around the
            control you are on. It is about 6 to 1 against the page by day and
            10 to 1 at night.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Controls are big enough to press</strong> on the reading
            pages: at least 36 pixels tall with a mouse and 44 on a touch
            screen.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Lines are 45 to 75 characters long</strong>, the range
            typographers and Bible publishers agree on. A larger text size
            makes lines shorter, never longer.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-motion">
        <h2 id="colophon-motion">Motion</h2>
        <p>
          Things move only to show where something came from, and then get out
          of the way. There are four speeds, all on one curve that starts fast
          and settles gently:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Instant</strong> for what you do all day: pointing, pressing, choosing a tab, turning a setting on.</li>
          <li><strong>Quick</strong> (0.12 seconds) for menus, popovers, and tooltips.</li>
          <li><strong>Settled</strong> (0.18 seconds) for notes, panels, and bars.</li>
          <li><strong>Large</strong> (0.26 seconds) for sheets and dialogs.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Nothing bounces. If your device is set to reduce motion, the site
          turns its movement off.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-js">
        <h2 id="colophon-js">Reading without JavaScript</h2>
        <p>
          Reading does not depend on JavaScript. Every chapter, introduction,
          article, and glossary entry is an ordinary page that loads and reads with
          JavaScript off. The tools that act on what you read, such as the
          Display settings, the verse menu, and search, need it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-built">
        <h2 id="colophon-built">What it is built with</h2>
        <p>
          The site is made with Astro, which builds every page ahead of time,
          so what reaches you is plain HTML and CSS. Its JavaScript is written
          by hand, with no framework. It is hosted on Cloudflare.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are no ads, and nothing is sold. The site counts page views
          with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which uses no cookies and does not
          follow you from site to site. The details are in our{" "}
          <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-credits">
        <h2 id="colophon-credits">Credits and licenses</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Fraunces, Source Sans 3, Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, OpenDyslexic,
            Noto Sans Hebrew (for Hebrew), and Cardo (for Greek and Hebrew in
            headings) are each used under the
            <a href="https://openfontlicense.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SIL Open Font License</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The icons are from
            <a href="https://lucide.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lucide</a>,
            used under the ISC license.
          </li>
          <li>
            The translation’s own license, and the credits for the source text,
            are on the <a href="/license/">license page</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="colophon-report">
        <h2 id="colophon-report">Tell us when something is wrong</h2>
        <p>
          If something here is hard to see, hard to press, or does not work for
          you, please tell us, and say what device or assistive technology you
          use. Write through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
